"Score each option 1-5 on each criterion. There is no NA: a field that says \"none\" or \"none known\" scores 1. Give each score a reason of at most 20 words. Score from the quantities, sources and gaps the option names; ignore verdict words such as COMPUTE-BOUND or NOT COMPUTE-BOUND. Also label each option SAME, ADJACENT or ELSEWHERE against the root question, with a reason: SAME if it asks the root question or a sub-question of it, ADJACENT if it has the root's subject but asks a different question, ELSEWHERE if its subject is different. Decide the subject from the title and summary; count root_link only if the summary itself describes the root question's object of study. Do not add up the scores. Decide only from the text below; do not use tools.
Root question: generative pipeline for discovery of single phase immersion coolants
Criteria:
stakes: Suppose the question this option raises were answered, either way. 5 = a choice someone faces (what to buy, build, collect or scale next) turns on the answer, and either answer would move it; 3 = the answer would refine a belief but no choice turns on it, or one answer is already much more likely than the other; 1 = either answer is already expected, or nothing would change. Do not score how famous or important the field is, or how close it is to the root question.
open: Has published work already measured how the answer changes with the quantity compute buys here (samples, ensemble members, resolution, model runs, model size, search width), across the range that $1,000 of Lambda on-demand GPU time (for scale, about 250 H100-hours) can reach? 5 = names the quantity and where published measurements stop (or that none exist), and the unmeasured range starts within that reach; 3 = the unmeasured range is reachable only through a smaller stand-in system, or published work already covers part of the reachable range; 1 = names no compute-bought quantity, or published work already covers everything within that reach, so the open range lies only beyond it.
reshape: Does the option name a part of the work that one answer waits on step after step, and a reason those steps might not have to run in order? 5 = names that chain and roughly how long it is; one answer leaves most of a GPU idle (a small state, or few runs needed, so copies side by side would not fill it); names what would have to be true of the system for the steps to be computed at the same time, in a form a cheap measurement could check; and no cited source already computes this chain in parallel for this system; 3 = names the chain and a way to restructure it, but one of these fails: the answer needs many independent runs, so copies side by side already fill the hardware; one run already fills a GPU; or no checkable condition is named; 1 = sequential says none, or the only parallelism is independent pieces (copies, a sweep, one job per GPU), or a cited source already computes this chain in parallel for this system. A method published for a different system does not lower this score. Do not score how famous the method or field is.
Options, one per line:
{\"id\":\"O\",\"title\":\"Generative search for single-phase immersion coolants\",\"summary\":\"Data-centre immersion cooling needs dielectric liquids with low viscosity, high thermal conductivity and heat capacity, a low dielectric constant and a high flash point. PFAS rules and the end of Novec production are pushing the field towards PFAS-free hydrocarbons and esters. Study whether a generative search over molecules and mixtures finds candidates that beat known commercial single-phase fluids on a cooling figure of merit. More compute buys more candidates checked by GPU molecular dynamics after predictor filtering, and longer simulations per candidate. Score against measured viscosity, thermal conductivity and dielectric data for known fluids held out of training, never against the predictors themselves.\",\"root_link\":\"SAME: a concrete version of the asker's line, kept on discovery and on transport properties (viscosity, thermal conductivity).\",\"compute_open\":\"Candidates checked by GPU molecular dynamics (about 10^2 to 10^4) and simulation length per candidate. Unknown: whether the hit rate against known commercial fluids keeps rising with more candidates, or whether predictor and force-field error caps it.\",\"sequential\":\"not given\"}
{\"id\":\"V1\",\"title\":\"Force-field error floor for coolant MD: an enlarged C10–C50 benchmark scored against measurement\",\"summary\":\"Data-centre immersion cooling needs dielectric liquids with low viscosity, high thermal conductivity and heat capacity, a low dielectric constant and a high flash point, and MD is increasingly used to rank candidates. Turn the MD verification step itself into the object of study: run one GPU-MD protocol for viscosity and thermal conductivity over a systematically enlarged set of C10–C50 hydrocarbons and esters, including the known commercial single-phase coolants, and score it only against measured values. More compute buys more benchmark molecules (from the handful used today to hundreds) and longer trajectories per molecule, not more novel candidates. Report the force-field error distribution and whether MD ranks known fluids in the same order as measurement well enough to trust a candidate ranking.\",\"root_link\":\"SAME: transport properties (viscosity, thermal conductivity) of organic liquids, verified by GPU-MD against measurement.\",\"compute_open\":\"Benchmark molecules covered (roughly 8 to a few hundred) and simulation length per molecule. Unknown: whether the error is a correctable systematic bias or a random floor that caps how far a ranking can be trusted.\",\"sequential\":\"not given\"}
{\"id\":\"V2\",\"title\":\"MD-ranked coolant shortlist judged against a self-driving lab's measured property panel\",\"summary\":\"Data-centre immersion cooling needs dielectric liquids with low viscosity, high thermal conductivity and heat capacity, a low dielectric constant and a high flash point, and PFAS rules plus the end of Novec production push the field to PFAS-free hydrocarbons and esters. Generate candidates, ML-filter to a ranked shortlist, and MD-verify the top 100, 300 and 1000 for viscosity, thermal conductivity, density, heat capacity and dielectric constant at the conditions a self-driving coolant lab measures. Judge the result by how well the MD ranking agrees with that lab's measured panel on overlapping candidates, and by how many MD-top candidates the lab must test to find one beating commercial fluids. Score never against the predictors themselves.\",\"root_link\":\"SAME: discovery of single-phase coolants judged on transport and dielectric properties, now anchored to measured data from a self-driving lab.\",\"compute_open\":\"Candidates MD-verified (10^2 to 10^3) and simulation length per candidate. Unknown: whether an MD ranking transfers to a wet-lab measured panel, or diverges on exactly the candidates that matter.\",\"sequential\":\"not given\"}
{\"id\":\"V3\",\"title\":\"MD verification inserted into Org-Mol's ester shortlist before synthesis\",\"summary\":\"Data-centre immersion cooling needs dielectric liquids with low viscosity, high thermal conductivity and heat capacity, and PFAS rules are pushing the field towards PFAS-free esters. Restrict the generative search to synthetically accessible PFAS-free esters of the kind already ML-screened at scale, filter to a ranked shortlist, and MD-verify the top 100, 300 then 1000 candidates' viscosity and thermal conductivity before any synthesis decision. Score against measured data for known esters and commercial single-phase fluids held out of training, never against the predictors. More compute buys more MD-verified candidates and longer simulations per candidate, testing whether the best cooling figure of merit keeps climbing beyond the 461-candidate shortlist stage.\",\"root_link\":\"SAME: ester discovery kept on transport properties and on the down-selection step where prior work stops.\",\"compute_open\":\"MD-verified candidates (100–1000, anchored to a ~461-candidate shortlist) and simulation length per candidate. Unknown: whether MD meaningfully reorders an ML shortlist or merely confirms it.\",\"sequential\":\"not given\"}

Return only one JSON value, with no other text, that matches this JSON Schema:
